# Line endings. The repository has always stored text as LF; this file is about
# what a CHECKOUT produces, which until now was CRLF on Windows via
# core.autocrlf=true. That divergence is why a file could parse in CI and fail
# on a clone — see the shebang in verifyPasswordPolicy.mjs, which vite hoists
# imports past, leaving `#!` mid-line where rolldown rejects it. LF only in CI
# meant LF only where anyone was looking.
#
# eol=lf, not bare text=auto: text=auto alone normalizes the INDEX, which is
# already clean, and leaves core.autocrlf to smudge CRLF back on checkout. Only
# eol=lf changes the working tree, and the working tree is the whole problem.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary. Every 0x0D byte in this repository lives in one of these; none of it
# is a line ending. text=auto's heuristic already classifies them correctly —
# these lines are here so that a future file type added alongside them inherits
# an explicit rule rather than a guess.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jar binary

# Windows batch files must keep CRLF. cmd.exe reads gradlew.bat line by line to
# resolve `goto` against its labels (:execute, :fail, :omega); with LF endings
# that parsing misbehaves. This is the one file in the repo that a checkout
# genuinely must NOT normalize, and CI cannot catch a regression here because
# ci.yml deliberately runs no Gradle step — it would break only on Windows,
# silently, which is the same failure shape this file exists to close.
*.bat text eol=crlf
